task.bug.news_article_loading_problem (completed) {
  bug:
  - news article: https://www.theinfatuation.com/san-francisco/guides/best-hot-pot-restaurants-san-francisco
  expect: the full news text content should appear in the news detail modal
  actual: only part of the article content is loaded

  Root cause:
  extractArticleParagraphs() returned the first XPath candidate with at least
  two paragraphs. On The Infatuation the <article> element contains only photo
  credits and author bios (5 short lines), while the actual guide body is
  rendered in a sibling container under <main>. The function stopped at the
  bylines, so the detail modal showed only author metadata. The page also
  leaves large CSS payloads inside <p> tags, which polluted broad candidates.

  Implemented:
  news_fetch.php
  - Article extraction now collects every candidate container first and picks
  the one with the greatest readable prose volume. A semantic container in
  the preferred order still wins when it holds a real body (at least
  ARTICLE_MIN_BODY_CHARS) or accounts for most of the best candidate, so
  pages with comments/related links do not lose their clean article
  container, while a byline-only <article> is skipped.
  - collectParagraphNodes() drops bare CSS/JS payloads (selector-prefixed text
  or text with multiple brace pairs) so they no longer count as prose.
  - Added an ARTICLE_EXTRACTOR_VERSION marker to cached article entries;
  entries from the old extractor are refetched instead of serving a stale
  partial body for up to six hours.

  Verified: the reported Infatuation URL now extracts 39 paragraphs / 9,048
  characters (intro, every restaurant entry, and related links) instead of 5
  bylines. Spot-checked full-article extraction against live NPR, CBC,
  Yahoo Finance, and KQED stories; all still return their complete bodies.
  php -l passes.
}

